c(1, "header lines 1-6; §2.1 table (verbatim); §2.2 table (verbatim); §2.3 table (verbatim)", "positioning", "Habit Streak | Daily Tracker (App Store ID 6504493690) by Adrien Blanc (com.adrienblc.habitstracker), Productivity / Utilities — a deliberately minimal daily habit tracker built around a streak counter ('fire' + consecutive days), Home Screen widget, calendar view, completion-percentage stats, reminders, emoji per habit; free tier capped at a habit count (3, then 5); data stored on device, no sign-up or cloud; 41 reviews (every one read), 18 storefronts, 2024-07-02 → 2026-07-15, mean 4.00 (25×5★, 5×4★, 2×3★, 4×2★, 5×1★); external: released 2024-07-01, version 1.72.0 (2026-04-19), free, 22 localisations; capabilities (verbatim) " + table("## 2.1 What the product is", 0) + " — absent per reviewers: social / friends, journaling, per-completion timestamps, retroactive completion, widget-initiated completion, Lock Screen widget, streak-milestone rewards; SKUs (verbatim) " + table("## 2.2 The listed model", 0) + " — six of nine SKUs state no period, the description names only 1-month and 1-year subscriptions with a 7-day trial yet a 'Premium Lifetime $79.99' exists, and neither surface states the free habit cap; free / paid (verbatim) " + table("## 2.3 Free / paid / trial / unclear", 0) + " — 'not one person names a premium capability other than more habits' — 'the paywall is being read as a quantity gate rather than a value gate'", "habit cap 3 → 5; 9 SKUs $1.99–$79.99", "mixed", "41 reviews; mean 4.00; public 451 ratings @ 4.67", "none", "header", "app-specific", ["12541530832","13744942868","11545888660"])
c(2, "How to read this; Seven warnings 1, 2, 3, 6, 7; §1.1 table (verbatim); §1.2 table (verbatim); §1.3 table (verbatim); §1.4; §1.5; §1.6; §1.7; §1.9 table (verbatim)", "data-caveat", "Method and limits: 154 code assignments (3.76 per review), 68 codes in 12 families built bottom-up, each with the quoted phrase that drove it, re-read twice; 25 integrity checks (verbatim) " + table("## 1.9 Verification performed after writing", 0) + " — checks 20 and 21 are the corpus independently confirming two dated external events (every cap-3 report predates every cap-5 report; every widget request predates every widget confirmation); files (verbatim) " + table("## 1.1 Files used", 0) + "; schema (verbatim) " + table("## 1.2 Schema actually present", 0) + "; coverage (verbatim) " + table("## 1.3 Coverage and reconciliation", 0) + " — 86 storefronts swept, 68 empty including gb and jp which hold public ratings; n = 41, one review 2.44% — the band table is 'close to useless here': the smallest non-zero share is already 'Meaningful' and three reviews read as 'High-priority', so Wilson 95% CIs accompany every headline and no single-review theme is promoted to a roadmap recommendation; no storefront reaches 50 (fr, de 10 each); no version field — eras by review date against dated releases; the paywall introduction date is inferred (Apple exposes no release before v1.31.0, 2025-01-30); 24 of 41 not in English, 10 written in English from non-English storefronts; segment rates labelled", "n/a", "mixed", "41 read; 0 eligible; one review = 2.44%", "research", "method", "generalisable", ["11929165753"])
c(3, "Warning 4, 5; §1.6 #5; §4.1 table (verbatim); §5.1 table (verbatim); §5.5; §8.6 #1, #3, #6; part 8 #1; part 8 #3; part 8 #6", "data-caveat", "Nobody in this corpus states that they bought the product: zero 'I bought / subscribed / upgraded / renewed / paid / got a refund' in six languages (read and regex-tested); one used a trial; evidence that exists (verbatim) " + table("## 5.1 The finding that governs", 0) + " — no conversion, trial-to-paid, renewal, refund or churn rate derivable; no post-purchase data at all — 'a genuine blank, not a clean bill of health'; text reviewers are not the rating base — 41 reviews on 451 public ratings with a ratings-weighted mean of 4.67 vs 4.00 (−0.67; France −1.34: 3.40 vs 4.74); distribution (verbatim) " + table("## 4.1 Distribution", 0) + " — research: conversion rate (#1), refund / cancellation / renewal rates (#3), what the 410 who rated without writing think (#6)", "n/a", "none", "0 purchasers; −0.67★ selection gap", "research", "research", "generalisable", ["12169201015","13237903873","14095584857"])
c(4, "§0.1 table (verbatim); §4.6; §4.7", "insight", "The product is liked; the pricing structure is what people write about: 36 of 41 (87.80%, Wilson 74.5–94.7%) contain explicit praise — including 9 of the 12 money complaints and 4 of the 5 one-star reviews (verbatim) " + table("## 0.1 The product is liked", 0) + " — 5 of 5 1★ (100%) are about money and nothing else; not one names a bug, missing feature, crash, data loss or support failure; four praise the product in the same sentence ('L'application est bien, mais dommage qu'elle soit devenue payante'); 'the rating damage in this corpus is not being caused by the product. It is being caused by where the free/paid line sits and how it is disclosed'; segment: monetization is 8 of the 11 reviews at 1–3★ (72.7%; 8/41 = 19.51%); the cap is the one theme that reliably predicts a low rating (cap objection 2.25, MON_CAP_5 1.00) while design and price themes span 1★ to 5★", "habit cap", "1★-burst", "praise 36 (87.80%); 5/5 1★ money; money-negative 12 (29.27%) 2.58", "product-rule", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["11740258271","12293956311","13421279860","13802989713","13871805750"])
